# Cursor Rules – ULS Components (UI Library)

## Project Context

- **Project Name**: ULS Components
- **Type**: Custom UI Component Library (Design System)
- **Purpose**: Generic, configurable, reusable components for multiple applications
- **Critical Rule**: This is a **library**, not an application
  - Components must be **generic** and **framework-agnostic** in logic
  - No application-specific logic or assumptions
  - No hardcoded business rules
  - No dependencies on specific app contexts

---

## Core Stack

### Framework & Version
- **Framework**: Ember.js
- **Version**: 6.4 LTS
- **Component Format**: `.gjs` (GlimmerJS single-file components)
- **Component Style**: Modern Glimmer components (Octane+)
- **Addon Structure**: Standard Ember addon conventions

### Component Naming
- **Prefix**: `uls-` (all components)
- **Examples**: 
  - `uls-button`
  - `uls-input-text`
  - `uls-tabmenu`
  - `uls-data-table`
- **File Naming**: `kebab-case.gjs`
- **Class Naming**: `PascalCase` (e.g., `UlsButton`)

---

## Component Philosophy

### PrimeReact Reference (Conceptual Only)
- **Purpose**: Use PrimeReact as a **conceptual reference** for:
  - Component responsibilities and scope
  - Interaction patterns and UX behavior
  - Props/arguments structure and naming
  - Accessibility requirements
  - Component categorization (elements, collections, modules)

### Implementation Requirements
- ✅ **MUST**: Write all components natively in Ember.js
- ✅ **MUST**: Use Ember idioms and patterns
- ✅ **MUST**: Follow Glimmer reactivity model (`@tracked`, `@action`)
- ✅ **MUST**: Use Ember modifiers for DOM interactions
- ❌ **NEVER**: Import or use PrimeReact code
- ❌ **NEVER**: Translate React code directly
- ❌ **NEVER**: Add PrimeReact as a dependency
- ❌ **NEVER**: Use React-specific patterns (hooks, JSX, etc.)

### Component Categories (Atomic Design)
- **Elements** (Atoms): Basic building blocks (`uls-button`, `uls-input`)
- **Collections** (Molecules): Composed components (`uls-card`, `uls-accordion`)
- **Modules** (Organisms): Complex components (`uls-dialog`, `uls-data-table`)

---

## Code Standards

### Component Structure
```javascript
// 1. Imports
import Component from '@glimmer/component';
import { tracked } from '@glimmer/tracking';
import { action } from '@ember/object';

// 2. Component class
export default class UlsComponentName extends Component {
  // 3. Services (if needed)
  // @service router;

  // 4. Tracked properties (minimal, only when needed)
  @tracked internalState = false;

  // 5. Computed getters
  get computedValue() {
    return this.args.value || 'default';
  }

  // 6. Actions
  @action
  handleAction() {
    // Action logic
  }

  // 7. Template
  <template>
    {{! Component template }}
  </template>
}
```

### Code Quality Requirements
- ✅ **Modular**: Single responsibility per component
- ✅ **Reusable**: No hardcoded values or app-specific logic
- ✅ **Maintainable**: Clear, readable, well-documented
- ✅ **Library-grade**: Production-ready, tested, documented
- ✅ **Framework-agnostic logic**: Business logic should be pure JS where possible

### State Management
- **Prefer**: Named arguments (`@args`) for external data
- **Use**: `@tracked` only for internal component state
- **Avoid**: Storing app-level state in components
- **Avoid**: Side effects or hidden state mutations

### Template Guidelines
- Use Handlebars syntax (not JSX)
- Leverage `...attributes` for flexible HTML attributes
- Use `{{yield}}` for content projection
- Use `{{yield (hash ...)}}` for named blocks
- Prefer `{{on "event" this.action}}` over inline handlers

### DOM Manipulation
- ✅ **Prefer**: Ember modifiers (`ember-modifier`)
- ✅ **Use**: `{{on}}` modifier for event handling
- ❌ **Avoid**: Direct DOM manipulation
- ❌ **Avoid**: `document.querySelector` in components
- **Exception**: Only when absolutely necessary (e.g., third-party library integration)

---

## Accessibility & UX

### Accessibility Requirements
- ✅ **MUST**: Be accessible by default
- ✅ **MUST**: Support keyboard navigation
- ✅ **MUST**: Include appropriate ARIA roles and attributes
- ✅ **MUST**: Support screen readers
- ✅ **MUST**: Follow WCAG 2.1 Level AA guidelines

### Keyboard Navigation
- Tab order must be logical
- All interactive elements must be keyboard accessible
- Escape key should close modals/overlays
- Arrow keys for navigation (menus, lists, etc.)

### ARIA Guidelines
- Use semantic HTML where possible
- Add ARIA attributes when semantic HTML isn't sufficient
- Ensure `aria-label`, `aria-describedby`, `aria-invalid` are used appropriately
- Maintain proper `role` attributes

### UX Consistency
- Follow PrimeReact interaction patterns (conceptually)
- Ensure consistent behavior across similar components
- Provide clear visual feedback for user actions
- Support loading and error states

---

## Documentation Requirements

### Component Documentation Template
Every component file must include documentation at the end:

```javascript
/**
 * ============================================================================
 * COMPONENT DOCUMENTATION
 * ============================================================================
 * 
 * Component: UlsComponentName
 * Category: [elements|collections|modules]
 * 
 * PURPOSE:
 * Brief description of what this component does and when to use it.
 * 
 * PUBLIC API (Arguments):
 * - @value {string} - Description of argument
 * - @onChange {Function} - Callback function description
 * - @disabled {boolean} - Whether component is disabled
 * 
 * INTERNAL LOGIC:
 * - Tracks internal state for [purpose]
 * - Handles [specific behavior]
 * 
 * ACCESSIBILITY:
 * - Keyboard navigation: [description]
 * - ARIA roles: [list roles]
 * - Screen reader support: [notes]
 * 
 * USAGE:
 * <UlsComponentName 
 *   @value={{this.value}}
 *   @onChange={{this.handleChange}}
 * />
 * 
 * CONSTRAINTS:
 * - [Any limitations or requirements]
 * - [Browser compatibility notes]
 * 
 * ============================================================================
 */
```

### Required Documentation Sections
1. **Purpose**: What the component does
2. **Public API**: All `@args` with types and descriptions
3. **Internal Logic**: Complex state management or behavior
4. **Accessibility**: Keyboard, ARIA, screen reader support
5. **Usage**: Code examples
6. **Constraints**: Limitations, requirements, browser notes

---

## Naming & Consistency

### Component Naming
- **Match**: PrimeReact naming intent (not implementation)
- **Keep**: Consistent API patterns across similar components
- **Ensure**: Predictable behavior within component categories
- **Prefer**: Clarity and maintainability over brevity

### Argument Naming Conventions
- Use descriptive names: `@onChange` not `@onClick` (unless it's actually a click)
- Boolean props: `@disabled`, `@required`, `@readonly`
- Event handlers: `@onChange`, `@onFocus`, `@onBlur`
- Value props: `@value`, `@options`, `@items`
- Configuration: `@variant`, `@size`, `@placement`

### Consistency Rules
- Similar components should have similar APIs
- Event handler naming should be consistent (`onX` pattern)
- Boolean arguments should follow same patterns
- Size/variant options should be consistent across components

---

## Folder Structure

### Component Organization
```
addon/components/
├── -private/              # Internal components (not exported)
│   ├── base-input.gjs
│   └── overlay-backdrop.gjs
│
├── elements/             # Atoms (basic components)
│   ├── button.gjs
│   ├── input-text.gjs
│   └── ...
│
├── collections/          # Molecules (composed components)
│   ├── card.gjs          # Simple (flat)
│   ├── accordion/        # Complex (folder with subcomponents)
│   │   ├── index.gjs
│   │   └── item.gjs
│   └── ...
│
└── modules/              # Organisms (complex components)
    ├── dialog/            # Complex (folder)
    │   ├── index.gjs
    │   └── header.gjs
    └── ...
```

### File Structure Rules
- **Elements**: Always flat files (simple components)
- **Collections**: Flat for simple, folders for complex (2+ subcomponents)
- **Modules**: Mostly folders, flat only for simple ones
- **Private**: Always flat (internal helpers)

---

## Testing Requirements

### Test Structure
- Mirror component structure in `tests/integration/components/`
- One test file per component
- Test accessibility, keyboard navigation, and ARIA attributes
- Test all public API arguments
- Test edge cases and error states

### Test Naming
- `{component-name}-test.js`
- Use descriptive test names
- Group related tests with `module()`

---

## Cursor Enforcement Rules

### When Generating New Components
1. ✅ Always follow this document
2. ✅ Use `.gjs` format
3. ✅ Follow Atomic Design categorization
4. ✅ Include full documentation
5. ✅ Implement accessibility features
6. ❌ Reject React-specific patterns
7. ❌ Reject app-level assumptions
8. ❌ Reject PrimeReact code imports

### When Modifying Existing Components
1. ✅ Preserve public API stability
2. ✅ Maintain backward compatibility
3. ✅ Update documentation
4. ✅ Update tests
5. ❌ Do not introduce breaking changes without justification
6. ❌ Do not remove public API without deprecation

### Code Review Checklist
- [ ] Component follows Atomic Design category
- [ ] Uses `.gjs` format correctly
- [ ] No React/PrimeReact code
- [ ] Accessibility implemented
- [ ] Documentation complete
- [ ] Tests written/updated
- [ ] No app-specific logic
- [ ] Follows naming conventions

---

## Best Practices Summary

### DO ✅
- Write native Ember components
- Use Glimmer component patterns
- Follow Atomic Design structure
- Implement accessibility by default
- Document thoroughly
- Keep components generic and reusable
- Use Ember modifiers for DOM interactions
- Prefer `@tracked` for reactive state
- Use `@action` for event handlers

### DON'T ❌
- Import or use PrimeReact code
- Add application-specific logic
- Use React patterns or JSX
- Skip accessibility features
- Skip documentation
- Hardcode values or assumptions
- Manipulate DOM directly
- Create tight coupling between components
- Introduce breaking changes without deprecation

